WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.252
NIST PQC standards use parameter sets and security categories to describe intended strength levels and operational choices.

2
00:00:08.252 --> 00:00:16.503
At a high level, a security category helps compare an algorithm parameter set to a target level of classical security strength.

3
00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:26.837
Practitioners do not need to perform the mathematics, but they do need to understand that parameter choices affect both security posture and system behavior.

4
00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:35.628
Parameter sets influence key sizes, ciphertext sizes, signature sizes, performance, memory use, bandwidth, and protocol overhead.

5
00:00:35.628 --> 00:00:40.178
Larger or stronger is not automatically better for every deployment.

6
00:00:40.178 --> 00:00:49.278
A high-volume service, a constrained device, a certificate-heavy workflow, and an offline signing process may all have different limits.

7
00:00:49.278 --> 00:00:57.221
A parameter choice that is acceptable in one environment may cause interoperability or performance problems in another.

8
00:00:57.221 --> 00:01:02.851
This is why PQC migration requires testing with real protocols and realistic traffic.

9
00:01:02.851 --> 00:01:05.936
The impact is not only the algorithm benchmark.

10
00:01:05.936 --> 00:01:20.048
It is the behavior of the whole system: handshake size, packet fragmentation, certificate chain size, firmware image size, hardware capacity, latency, memory pressure, logging, monitoring, and failure handling.

11
00:01:20.048 --> 00:01:23.673
Small assumptions can become large deployment issues.

12
00:01:23.673 --> 00:01:29.919
The practical approach is to match parameters to risk, protocol, and operational constraints.

13
00:01:29.919 --> 00:01:35.626
Start with standards-based options, then test in the environments where they will run.

14
00:01:35.626 --> 00:01:44.417
Security teams should bring architects, developers, infrastructure owners, device teams, and vendors into the conversation early.

15
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:48.659
PQC is cryptographic, but its tradeoffs are deeply operational.
